      "id": "GENUS_MANIS",
      "name": "Manis",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "Asian pangolins — four species found across South and Southeast Asia. Pangolins are the only mammals with true scales, made of keratin (the same protein as human fingernails and rhino horns). When threatened, they roll into a tight ball, with the overlapping scales creating near-impenetrable armour — lions and leopards have been recorded failing to unroll pangolins. Pangolins feed exclusively on ants and termites, which they excavate with powerful claws and collect with a tongue longer than their body (which anchors to their pelvis). They have no teeth; their muscular stomach grinds insects with ingested sand and small stones. Asian pangolins are classified as critically endangered — they are the world's most trafficked wild mammals, with an estimated one million taken from the wild in the 2000s for use in traditional medicine and as luxury food.",
      "lineage": "Asian Pangolins",
