      "id": "GENUS_PHATAGINUS",
      "name": "Phataginus",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "African tree pangolins — two small, arboreal species found in the rainforests of central and west Africa. Tree pangolins have longer tails relative to body length, with bare scales on the underside of the tail tip that allow gripping branches. The white-bellied pangolin is the most common African species and the most frequently encountered in markets. Tree pangolins are almost entirely nocturnal and arboreal, sleeping in tree hollows by day. Like all pangolins, they are solitary except when mating and when females are caring for young.",
